KW  - VEGETATION
KW  - INDEX
KW  - ALGORITHMS
AB  - Accurate estimation of aboveground biomass (AGB) is essential for evaluating the ecological functions of desert rangelands and supporting sustainable grazing management. Low-altitude unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) remote sensing has emerged as a powerful tool for surveying rangeland resources. However, the complex grassland environment and high data acquisition costs often lead to extremely limited AGB samples, which fail to meet the large-sample requirements of deep learning models. Moreover, most previous studies relied solely on spectral information, without effectively leveraging spatial contextual relationships, thereby restricting improvements in model accuracy. To address these challenges, this study proposes a cross-task collaborative learning network (CTCLNet) for AGB estimation from UAV-based hyperspectral imagery in desert rangelands. The method incorporates a vegetation species classification task to provide auxiliary supervisory signals to the feature extraction network, utilizing abundant classification samples to mitigate the inadequate feature learning caused by limited AGB samples. Furthermore, a dual-branch feature extraction architecture is designed to enhance the representation of vegetation community structure and biomass variation. Experimental results demonstrate that incorporating spatial neighborhood information can substantially improve AGB estimation accuracy. Compared with models based on handcrafted features, CTCLNet achieved the highest estimation performance, with R-2 = 0.94, RMSE = 18.58 g/m(2), and MAE = 14.67 g/m(2). Relative to models trained solely on the AGB dataset, CTCLNet increased R-2 by 22.08% and reduced RMSE by 48.97%. Additionally, significance analysis across different grazing zones indicated that grazing significantly reduced AGB (P < 0.05). This study offers a novel approach for AGB estimation under sample-limited conditions and provides technical support for the sustainable management of desert rangelands. The code is available at https://github.com/zhang2508/ CTCLNet.
