TI  - Multidimensional Validation of FVC Products over Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau Alpine Grasslands: Integrating Spatial Representativeness Metrics with Machine Learning Optimization
T2  - REMOTE SENSING
LA  - English
KW  - fractional vegetation cover
KW  - product validation
KW  - machine learning
KW  - Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau
AB  - Highlights What are the main findings? A framework synergizing spatial representativeness, ML tuning, and multidimensional validation enhances FVC product validation robustness. GEOV3 demonstrates superior accuracy compared to GLASS and MuSyQ FVC products on alpine grasslands. What are the implications of the main findings? The proposed framework provides a methodological basis for field sampling design and scale-matched validation of satellite FVC in heterogeneous landscapes. The comparative results can offer scientific guidance for selecting appropriate FVC products and improving retrieval algorithms for alpine ecosystem monitoring on the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau.Highlights What are the main findings? A framework synergizing spatial representativeness, ML tuning, and multidimensional validation enhances FVC product validation robustness. GEOV3 demonstrates superior accuracy compared to GLASS and MuSyQ FVC products on alpine grasslands. What are the implications of the main findings? The proposed framework provides a methodological basis for field sampling design and scale-matched validation of satellite FVC in heterogeneous landscapes. The comparative results can offer scientific guidance for selecting appropriate FVC products and improving retrieval algorithms for alpine ecosystem monitoring on the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau.Abstract Fractional Vegetation Cover (FVC) dynamics on the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau (QTP) are critical indicators for assessing ecosystem condition. However, uncertainties persist in the accuracy of existing FVC products over the QTP due to retrieval differences, scale effects, and limited validation data. This study utilized the Google Earth Engine platform to integrate unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) observations, Sentinel-2, MODIS, climate, and topography datasets, and proposed a comprehensive framework incorporating dual-index screening, machine learning optimization, and multidimensional validation to systematically assess the accuracy of GEOV3, GLASS, and MuSyQ FVC products in the alpine grasslands. The dual-index screening reduced validation uncertainty by improving the spatial representativeness of ground data. To build a high-precision evaluation dataset with limited inter-class coverage, recursive feature elimination and grid search were applied to optimize five ML models, and CatBoost achieved the superior performance (R2 = 0.880, RMSE = 0.122), followed by XGBoost, GBM, LightGBM, and RF models. Four validation scenarios were implemented, including direct validation using 250 m UAV plot FVC and multi-scale validation using a 10 m FVC reference aggregated to product grids. Results show that GEOV3 (R2 = 0.909-0.925, RMSE = 0.082-0.103) outperformed GLASS (R2 = 0.742-0.771, RMSE = 0.138-0.175) and MuSyQ (R2 = 0.739-0.746, RMSE = 0.138-0.181), both of which exhibited systematic underestimation. This framework significantly enhances FVC product validation reliability, providing a robust solution for remote sensing product validation in alpine grassland ecosystems.
