AB  - The increasing frequency of wildfires globally because of climate changing has resulted in significant casualties and substantial property losses for humanity. Due to the difficulty of extinguishing wildfires, establishing firebreaks in advance is a critical approach to impeding their rapid spread. However, conventional firebreaks such as setting non-vegetation regions or biologically fire prevention forests affect the natural evolution of vegetation, which is not conformed to the requirements of sustainable development. In this study, an eco-friendly fire retardant was developed for forest grassland firebreaks, which can be rapidly implemented by drones and without affecting vegetation growth. The rapid carbonization of polyhydroxyl compounds in vegetation was catalyzed by the abundant acidic sites in fire retardant. Therefore, total heat release and total smoke production were reduced by 30 % and 18.7 % respectively. In addition, establishing a fire spread model based on the Wildland-urban interface Fire Dynamics Simulator (WFDS) to validate the fire prevention effect of firebreaks. This work is aiming to serve as a fundamental basis for designing large-scale forest grassland fire prevention and firebreaks.
